From 62fb39339546a27ac42f3d363c3995d44e5d1d76 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 20:55:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 16/17] aeraui: take an RPC request whose writer already hung
 up

RunFrame polls AERA's RPC input FIFO for POLLIN only. When a frame reads
a request after its writer has written but before it closes,
HandleInput() buffers the bytes and waits for EOF. Once the writer
closes, a FIFO with no data left reports only POLLHUP, so the request
sits in the buffer until the next client writes; that client's bytes
are then appended and it gets the earlier request's answer. A shell
client (printf > /system/bin/aerain) hits the window often:

  plugin open: no answer for minutes, the counter opens only when the
  next request (op mirror) arrives, which then receives
  {"event":"log","id":"lab","text":"Opening counter\n"}
  and never runs itself.

Treat POLLHUP like POLLIN so the read sees the EOF and the request is
dispatched at once. Rearm() reopens the FIFO for every request, and
Linux reports no POLLHUP on a FIFO that no writer has opened since, so an
idle channel does not wake the loop.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01J6XhHxrkhAcXmkzHMeCnVz
---
 aeraui/core/engine.cpp | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/aeraui/core/engine.cpp b/aeraui/core/engine.cpp
index a82290d..77bf86a 100644
--- a/aeraui/core/engine.cpp
+++ b/aeraui/core/engine.cpp
@@ -461,7 +461,9 @@ public:
     const int rpc_fd = aera::rpc::Channel::InputFd();
     if (rpc_fd >= 0) {
       pollfd rpc{rpc_fd, POLLIN, 0};
-      if (poll(&rpc, 1, 0) > 0 && (rpc.revents & POLLIN))
+      // A writer that closes between two frames leaves only POLLHUP: the
+      // request is already buffered and its end is that hang-up.
+      if (poll(&rpc, 1, 0) > 0 && (rpc.revents & (POLLIN | POLLHUP)))
         aera::rpc::Channel::HandleInput();
     }
     const int cancel_fd = aera::rpc::Channel::CancelFd();
